## In a function component: useEffect

Data fetching is a side effect, so it goes in `useEffect`, not in the render body:

```jsx
function UserCard({ id }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    fetch(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then(setUser);
    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [id]);

  return user ? <h1>{user.name}</h1> : <p>Loading…</p>;
}
```

## Why the effect, and why cleanup

Fetching during render would fire on every render and is not allowed. An effect runs after the component is on screen — the equivalent of the old `componentDidMount`. The cleanup aborts the request so a fast unmount, or a changing `id`, does not set state on a gone component or apply a stale response.

## The dependency array

List everything the fetch depends on — here `[id]` — so it refetches when the input changes and not on unrelated renders.

## In practice

Most apps do not hand-write this. A data library — TanStack Query, SWR — adds caching, dedup, retries, and loading and error states. And in a Next.js App Router app, fetching usually happens in a Server Component, before anything reaches the client.
